title mycelium Lapse r3 — the deadline verdict reads across a Knot, the hour kept unbroken
prompt loop card: keep going Lindy-first crux-first; each finished increment its own signed round; send often
think r3 makes the Lapse's corpus a Knot (sealed committed prefix) plus its live suffix, mirroring pledge_knot/braid_knot. The crux is a deadline set before the checkpoint cut and reached after it: a pledge opens under deadline 2 inside the sealed prefix (genesis + pledge, base 2), stands open at the seal, and the suffix post rides absolute position base+0 = 2 — so the clock reaches the deadline in the suffix and the pledge LAPSES across the cut, coins home to Alice, the post a no-op.
think The honest seam the crux surfaced, genuinely new to the Lapse (the way braid_knot surfaced the tax-burn seam): the Pledge's timing is irrelevant to its verdict, so pledge_knot folds its suffix at LOCAL positions and says the pruned prefix's global order is simply gone. The Lapse cannot — its deadline is an ABSOLUTE order position, so the clock must not restart at the cut. So a LapseKnot carries one field more than a PledgeKnot: the epoch base, the count of order positions the prefix consumed (computed with cord.commit, the same number resolve walked). continue_lapse folds the suffix at base + i, so a deadline named before the cut fires at exactly the position it chose. Without the base, the deadline would be meaningless across a pruning — carrying it is what makes a deadline durable across a checkpoint.
think Proved the verdict never drifts: the whole Cord (genesis, pledge deadline 2, post) resolves to the same lapsed verdict as the Knot-plus-suffix; lapses_match compares posted/voided/lapsed counts, every balance and reserve, and each pledge's state, amount, AND deadline. A companion scene with deadline 5 honors the suffix post instead (a promise made before the cut, kept after it, under a deadline that had time), and a suffix void releases it — the two-phase verdict still reads across the cut, now under a live clock. Conservation holds past the cut on every path.
think Integrity across the cut, three ways (mirroring pledge_knot): a Knot corrupted after sealing refuses KnotCorrupt before a suffix fact is folded (a runtime verify gate, never an assert); a tampered suffix fact refuses IdentityRefused; a suffix resolution of a pledge the checkpoint never opened refuses UnknownPledge. Note a field named `void` collided with Zig's keyword in the seat helper's anon-struct return — renamed to void_block.
obs GREEN after the field rename; witness re-runs the Lapse and the Knot at the door. TAME bans clean, width-check clean. Additive — composes lapse + cord_knot + cord + fold public API only, editing none.
loom witness=mycelium_lapse_knot_witness build=ok selftest=GREEN base=2 lapsed_across=1 honored_far=1 no_drift=yes
file mycelium/lapse_knot.rye r3 — the deadline verdict across a Knot; LapseKnot carries the epoch base so the absolute clock is unbroken; a prefix-opened pledge lapses when the suffix reaches its deadline
file tools/mycelium_lapse_knot_witness.rish GREEN witness — proves lapse-across-the-cut, the epoch-base clock, no drift vs the whole Cord, the far-deadline honor, conservation past the cut, and every integrity refusal; the Lapse and the Knot stand at the door
recommend keep-going Lapse r4 closes the arc — a real reproducible on-disk fixture carrying a genuine lapsed reservation reads true (app == awk over the same bytes a keeper can walk), mirroring pledge_true/braid_true. A served lapse · per-account mint authority · breach renames (cairn-first) · deep debride · seed force-push stay the maintainer's hand.
